in 1923's the ten commandments the  israelites walk through the parted red  sea it's actually two separate shots  combined into one according to sources  at the time the first shot was just the  actors and the second was actually  gelatin  this compositing technique was a  precursor to the blue and green screen  technology that has taken over the  industry there's almost nothing that is  completely unrelated to a previous  effect back before the widespread use of  cgi effects artists and filmmakers did  everything from painting on top of shots  to projecting moving backgrounds to make  the impossible happen  here are seven tricks used in old  hollywood that still exist in some form  today  cg allows filmmakers to place people and  objects anywhere they want in a scene  but it wasn't used in a movie until  1973's west world so how did filmmakers  in the early 1900s do this  with arguably the first ever special  effect the substitution splice  a filmmaker would start filming a scene  then they'd stop the camera and start  shooting again from the same position  with a new person or object added to the  frame this effect made it seem like  something or someone appeared out of  thin air  director georges melies used a  substitution splice in many of his  on-screen illusions such as this one in  1896's the vanishing lady the trick was  also used to pull off dangerous stunts  take a look at 1895's the execution of  mary queen of scots to make the  monarch's beheading look real the actor  first knelt at the chopping block then  the director stopped filming and a dummy  was placed where the actor was when they  resumed filming the executioner chopped  off the dummy's head by today's  standards this early form of editing is  pretty noticeable  stunt coordinators these days improve  this concept with camera lock-offs take  this moment when the rock's character  gets hit in the face by a motorcycle in  2016's central intelligence to keep the  actor safe they filmed the shot from the  exact same angle twice first they  captured a motorcyclist doing a backflip  then the rock stunt double being pulled  back with today's visual effects  technology the two takes could be  seamlessly combined  one of the earliest challenges of  animation was natural movement so  animators turned to rotoscoping first  thought up by max and dave fleischer  artists would project live-action film  footage onto their easel and sketch over  the images frame by frame so they could  mimic real-life movements for example in  the 1932 cartoon mini the moocher the  fleischer brothers gave the characters  eye-popping dance moves by rotoscoping  cab callaway nowadays rotoscoping  software allows for this process to be  used on something much longer than a  short cartoon like the tv series undone  and motion capture tech is a natural  extension of the fleischer's work with  fully realized mocap characters like  thanos in infinity war  1933's the invisible man needed to shoot  its invisible lead wearing clothes so  the audience could see him how do you  create a floating shirt without today's  technology  instead of a green screen they used a  technique called compositing in melies  time this could be done through double  exposure but james whale instead used a  compositing method known as the williams  process  first after claude reigns was filmed in  black velvet against a black backdrop  this created a negative allowing every  part of the actor to disappear except  for the parts not covered in black they  then filmed the background separately  these maths were then printed masking  off the foreground and background until  the two images were combined  creating the right composite images  often involved thinking outside the box  like that scene from the ten  commandments we described earlier  because the compositing was done by hand  it was an imperfect process if you watch  old movies in which you can see the  edges around the figures where they've  been composited in that means that the  optical printer operator was short on  time oftentimes it's just chief effects  because they didn't want to spend money  to do it modern compositing is much  smoother the black screen used in 1933's  invisible man has been replaced with  today's blue and green screens and  elements are filmed on motion control  cameras for a more seamless composite  like in 2020 is the invisible man they  first shot one take of elizabeth moss  being attacked by a stand-in and then a  second clean take without them  things get a bit more complicated when  it comes to fantasy backgrounds like in  the wizard of oz the filmmakers couldn't  just composite in real footage for the  land of oz and building all the sets  from scratch would have been a difficult  task  so instead they used matte paintings to  paint around the characters  for this effect a map blocking  everything except the actors was placed  in front of the camera the artists later  painted the surroundings on a separate  glass mat before the two images were  combined so for example when dorothy  walks down the yellow brick road in this  shot it was actually just the actors on  a set with no backdrop the sky flowers  and mountains were all painted in after  the fact to make it look like they were  headed towards the emerald city but it's  still hard to overcome the flatness of a  painting look at this shot of orson  welles speaking in front of a large  crowd in citizen kane the painted extras  appear completely lifeless  present day digital mats and set  extensions offer a fix to that by  duplicating tiles of real extras and  adding digital doubles into the mix ted  lasso could create a fully formed  stadium filled with 26 000 fans who  could react directly to the action shot  in camera  without today's animation software it  can be difficult to add realistic  three-dimensional depth to hand-drawn  scenes so in the 1930s and 40s many  animated disney classics used an  invention called a multi-plane camera  the camera was attached to several  layers of glass each displaying a  different layer of artwork each layer  could be moved at different speeds and  distances from each other  because the two-dimensional drawings  were separated in real three-dimensional  space they created a parallax effect  this camera helped add depth to the  forest and bambi and it's how filmmakers  made the evil queen surrounding spin  around her in snow white in the seven  dwarfs  these days the multi-plane camera effect  is recreated digitally the various  layers can be designed and easily  separated on a computer to create the  illusion of depth and filmmakers are  getting more creative with how they  integrate 3d assets and trick the eye  2020s wolf walkers created a 3d forest  as a guide to place hand-drawn  characters into dynamic environments  even before cg filmmakers tried to fix  the static nature of painted mats and 2d  backgrounds one solution was rear  projection which created dynamic moving  backgrounds in real time an actor would  stand in front of a screen and behind  that screen a projector would play  footage shot elsewhere  like this shot from 1938's bringing up  baby in reality the two stars on this  leopard were nowhere near each other  harry grant and catherine hepburn were  actually driving in a fake car on a set  while the leopard was shot separately  and projected behind them on a screen  for this plane crash from 1940s foreign  correspondent director alfred hitchcock  projected real footage of the sky and  ocean through the cockpit's windows  but rear projection had its own issues  because it was projected from the back  the lighting and image looked a lot less  realistic plus it was difficult to match  the lighting of the real world in the  studio  that's why the background roads in these  driving scenes from to catch a thief and  dr no look so flat today  rear projection also became harder to  utilize as more and more films were shot  in color  colors are often just a little off and  they're just off enough that especially  today because you know with digital  composites you can correct that stuff  pretty easily but you couldn't at the  time still rear projection lives on  through boundary pushing technologies  used by directors like stanley kubrick  and john favreau  in 2001 a space odyssey kubrick instead  placed the projector in front of the  screen with the light bouncing off a  mirror this created a stunning deep  focus and perspective during the dawn of  man sequence you won't see that in a  rear projection or any map painting and  led screens have turned projection from  a cheesy effect to awe-inspiring  for example the mandalorian chose to  swap projections and green screens with  led screens you can immediately see how  naturally the light reflects off the  mandalorian and baby yoda in this shot  and because the camera actually moves in  sync with the background this shot has a  natural parallax effect just like those  multiplane cameras from decades earlier  before monsters and other creatures  could be created with cg and motion  capture filmmakers primarily used stop  motion animation but stop motion  requires expensive miniature models and  sets which often look unnatural when  interacting with the cast you can see  that from looking at king kong as the  claymation kong can't truly interact  with fey ray  so pioneering stop-motion animator ray  harryhausen thought of dynamation  his method combined a range of visual  effects techniques from maths to  projection  to understand how it works let's look at  this fight scene between human  characters and a skeleton army in 1963's  jason and the argonauts the actors  rehearsed their moves with stunt people  who stood in for the skeleton army but  the scene was actually shot without the  stunt performers and only with the  actors playing the human characters  erihausen then used stop motion for the  skeleton army to match the movements  perfectly the footage of the actor's  shadow boxing was projected behind the  animation table while harryhausen moved  the skeletons frame by frame a camera  filmed the skeleton army through a glass  mat masking off the shadow boxing actors  finally the shadow boxing scene and the  skeleton scenes were printed and  composited together this five-minute  sequence took four and a half months to  complete but dynamation's influence can  still be seen in the blue and green suit  performers standing in for characters  that will be created with cg in 2019's  dumbo a green suit performer helped the  cast with their sight lines when  interacting with the baby elephant and  motion capture allows an actor to play a  cg dog on set so nobody is guessing  where they have to look  looking back at dynamation's individual  elements each effect can seem  time-consuming and outdated on its own  but it shows how every technological  advancement in cinema wouldn't exist  without the low-tech experiments of the  industry's past  [Music]  you
 

